higher branches bifurcate from the branches of degenerate standing waves with vanishing tail outside the ring. Moreover, we analyze stability of bifurcating standing waves. Namely, we show that the primary branch is composed by orbitally stable standing waves for subcritical power nonlinearities, while all nontrivial higher branches are linearly unstable near the bifurcation point. The stability character of the degenerate branches remains inconclusive at the analytical level, whereas heuristic arguments based on analysis of embedded eigenvalues of negative Krein signatures support the conjecture of their linear instability at least near the bifurcation point. Numerical results for the cubic NLS equation show that this conjecture is valid and that the degenerate branches become spectrally stable far away from the bifurcation point.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForbidden hypermatrices imply general bounds on (https://www.math.kit.edu/iag6/lehre/extprobcomb2020w/media/palvolgyi-induced-poset.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Forbidden hypermatrices imply general bounds on ... is that we associate a d-dimensional 0 −1 hypermatrix to Q and we establish a connection to the ... Tardos theorem [25] about forbidden submatrices. ... Research supported by Hungarian National Science Fund\nForbidden hypermatrices imply general bounds on\ninduced forbidden subposet problems\nAbhishek Methuku∗\nD¨om¨ot¨or P´alv¨olgyi†\nMarch 28, 2018\nAbstract\nWe prove that for every poset P, there is a constant C such that the size of any family\nof subsets of [n] that does not contain P as an induced subposet is at most C\n\u0000 n\n⌊n\n2 ⌋\n\u0001\n, settling a\nconjecture of Katona, and Lu and Milans [24]. We obtain this bound by establishing a connection\nto the theory of forbidden submatrices and then applying a higher dimensional variant of the\nMarcus-Tardos theorem, proved by Klazar and Marcus. We also give a new proof of their result.\n1\nIntroduction\nWe are interested in the largest family of subsets of [n] := {1, 2, . . . , n} avoiding certain subposets.\nOur main result is the following.\nTheorem 1. For every poset P, there is a constant C such that the size of any family of subsets\nof [n] that does not contain an induced copy of P is at most C\n\u0000 n\n⌊n\n2 ⌋\n\u0001\n.\nTo establish this theorem, we use a method that is a certain generalization of the so-called circle\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nh-POLYNOMIALS OF REDUCTION TREES (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=f63daa0b534fd1c69295bc62c81e6bddc2106361&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn9search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; be read oﬀfrom the corresponding reduction tree in the subdivision algebra. ... notion of shellable reduction trees in the subdivision and related algebras and deﬁne h-polynomials ... reduction trees in the subdivision algebra equal the h-polynomials of shellable triangulations of ﬂow ... The author was partially supported by a National Science Foundation Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (DMS\nh-POLYNOMIALS OF REDUCTION TREES\nKAROLA M´ESZ´AROS\nAbstract. Reduction trees are a way of encoding a substitution procedure dictated by the relations\nof an algebra. We use reduction trees in the subdivision algebra to construct canonical triangulations\nof ﬂow polytopes which are shellable. We explain how a shelling of the canonical triangulation can\nbe read oﬀfrom the corresponding reduction tree in the subdivision algebra. We then introduce the\nnotion of shellable reduction trees in the subdivision and related algebras and deﬁne h-polynomials\nof reduction trees.\nIn the case of the subdivision algebra, the h-polynomials of the canonical\ntriangulations of ﬂow polytopes equal the h-polynomials of the corresponding reduction trees, which\nmotivated our deﬁnition. We show that the reduced forms in various algebras, which can be read oﬀ\nfrom the leaves of the reduction trees, specialize to the shifted h-polynomials of the corresponding\nreduction trees. This yields a technique for proving nonnegativity properties of reduced forms. As\na corollary we settle a conjecture of A.N. Kirillov.\nContents\n1.\nIntroduction\n1\n2.\nDeﬁnitions and Prelimiaries\n2\n2.1.\nFlow polytopes and their subdivisions.\n2\n2.2.\nAlgebras related to ﬂow polytopes.\n4\n3.\nWeak embeddability and shelling triangulations\n5\n4.\nStrong embeddability and a description of the leaves of RO\nG\n10\n5.\nReﬁning h-vectors of the canonical triangulation of F eG\n13\n6.\nThe weak and strong embeddable properties of partial reduction trees\n15\n7.\nSolving Kirillov’s Conjecture 7\n17\n8.\nThe proof of Theorem 4\n18\n8.1.\nIntersection of ﬂow polytopes as intersection of graphs.\n20\nAcknowledgement\n23\nReferences\n23\n1. Introduction\narXiv:1407.2684v1  [math.CO]  10 Jul 2014\nReduction trees are a way of encoding a substitution procedure dictated by the relations of\nan algebra. Reduced forms can be read oﬀfrom reduction trees. We establish a framework for\nstudying reduced forms in several related algebras. We accomplish this by introducing a notion\nalike shellability for reduction trees (which we call strong embeddability), inspired by the geometric\nnotion. We also deﬁne h-polynomials of reduction trees. The h-polynomials of certain shellable\nreduction trees in the subdivision algebra equal the h-polynomials of shellable triangulations of ﬂow\npolytopes, which was the motivation for our deﬁnition. We show that the reduced forms in various\nalgebras specialize to the shifted h-polynomials of the corresponding reduction trees. This yields\na technique for proving nonnegativity results for reduced forms. We demonstrate the geometric\nDate: July 9, 2014.\nThe author was partially supported by a National Science Foundation Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (DMS\n1103933).\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1407.2684] h-Polynomials of Reduction Trees (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fabs%2F1407.2684%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; We explain how a shelling of the canonical triangulation can be read off from the corresponding reduction tree in the subdivision algebra. ... In the case of the subdivision algebra, the h-polynomials of the canonical triangulations of flow polytopes equal the h-polynomials of the corresponding reduction trees, which motivated our definition.\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 10 Jul 2014 (v1), last revised 13 Feb 2015 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:h-Polynomials of Reduction Trees\n\nAuthors:Karola Mészáros\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled h-Polynomials of Reduction Trees, by Karola M\\'esz\\'aros\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Reduction trees are a way of encoding a substitution procedure dictated by the relations of an algebra. We use reduction trees in the subdivision algebra to construct canonical triangulations of flow polytopes which are shellable. We explain how a shelling of the canonical triangulation can be read off from the corresponding reduction tree in the subdivision algebra. We then introduce the notion of shellable reduction trees in the subdivision and related algebras and define h-polynomials of reduction trees. In the case of the subdivision algebra, the h-polynomials of the canonical triangulations of flow polytopes equal the h-polynomials of the corresponding reduction trees, which motivated our definition. We show that the reduced forms in various algebras, which can be read off from the leaves of the reduction trees, specialize to the shifted h-polynomials of the corresponding reduction trees. This yields a technique for proving nonnegativity properties of reduced forms. As a corollary we settle a conjecture of A.N. Kirillov.\n\nComments:  | 23 pages, 3 figures\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:1407.2684 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:1407.2684v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1407.2684 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\nFrom: Karola Meszaros [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 10 Jul 2014 04:12:40 UTC (111 KB)\n[v2] Fri, 13 Feb 2015 14:28:54 UTC (107 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForbidden hypermatrices imply general bo... (https://axi.lims.ac.uk/paper/1408.4093)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; # Forbidden hypermatrices imply general bounds on induced forbidden subposet problems ... We obtain this bound by establishing a connection to the theory of forbidden submatrices and then applying a higher dimensional variant of the Marcus-Tardos theorem, proved by Klazar and Marcus.\n\nID: 1408.4093 ID: 1408.4093\n\nSearch\n\n# Forbidden hypermatrices imply general bounds on induced forbidden subposet problems\n\nAugust 18, 2014\n\nView on ArXiv\n\nAbhishek Methuku, Dömötör Pálvölgyi\n\nMathematics\n\nCombinatorics\n\nWe prove that for every poset $P$, there is a constant $C$ such that the size of any family of subsets of $[n]$ that does not contain $P$ as an induced subposet is at most $C{\\binom{n}{\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor}}$, settling a conjecture of Katona, and Lu and Milans. We obtain this bound by establishing a connection to the theory of forbidden submatrices and then applying a higher dimensional variant of the Marcus-Tardos theorem, proved by Klazar and Marcus. We also give a new proof of their result.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndc_1942_21\n\n[33] Jerrold R Griggs – Linyuan Lu: On (https://real-d.mtak.hu/1456/1/dc_1942_21_tezisek%20%282%29.pdf)\nciteturn9search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; *Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A*, 25. évf. ... [44] Abhishek Methuku – Döm